.br
.SS "Globbing features"
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBasic globbing\fP (Wildcard matching)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBAdvanced globbing\fP (extglobs, posix brackets, brace matching)
.PP
.SS "Basic globbing"
\fBCharacter\fP  \fBDescription\fP  \fR*\fP  Matches any character zero or more times, excluding path separators\&. Does \fInot match\fP path separators or hidden files or directories ("dotfiles"), unless explicitly enabled by setting the \fRdot\fP option to \fRtrue\fP\&.  \fR**\fP  Matches any character zero or more times, including path separators\&. Note that \fR**\fP will only match path separators (\fR/\fP, and \fR\\\\\fP on Windows) when they are the only characters in a path segment\&. Thus, \fRfoo**/bar\fP is equivalent to \fRfoo*/bar\fP, and \fRfoo/a**b/bar\fP is equivalent to \fRfoo/a*b/bar\fP, and \fImore than two\fP consecutive stars in a glob path segment are regarded as \fIa single star\fP\&. Thus, \fRfoo/***/bar\fP is equivalent to \fRfoo/*/bar\fP\&.  \fR?\fP  Matches any character excluding path separators one time\&. Does \fInot match\fP path separators or leading dots\&.  \fR[abc]\fP  Matches any characters inside the brackets\&. For example, \fR[abc]\fP would match the characters \fRa\fP, \fRb\fP or \fRc\fP, and nothing else\&.  
.SS "Matching behavior vs\&. Bash"
Picomatch's matching features and expected results in unit tests are based on Bash's unit tests and the Bash 4\&.3 specification, with the following exceptions:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Bash will match \fRfoo/bar/baz\fP with \fR*\fP\&. Picomatch only matches nested directories with \fR**\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Bash greedily matches with negated extglobs\&. For example, Bash 4\&.3 says that \fR!(foo)*\fP should match \fRfoo\fP and \fRfoobar\fP, since the trailing \fR*\fP bracktracks to match the preceding pattern\&. This is very memory-inefficient, and IMHO, also incorrect\&. Picomatch would return \fRfalse\fP for both \fRfoo\fP and \fRfoobar\fP\&.
.PP

.PP

.br
.SS "Advanced globbing"
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBextglobs\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBPOSIX brackets\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBraces\fP
.PP
.SS "Extglobs"
\fBPattern\fP  \fBDescription\fP  \fR@(pattern)\fP  Match \fIonly one\fP consecutive occurrence of \fRpattern\fP  \fR*(pattern)\fP  Match \fIzero or more\fP consecutive occurrences of \fRpattern\fP  \fR+(pattern)\fP  Match \fIone or more\fP consecutive occurrences of \fRpattern\fP  \fR?(pattern)\fP  Match \fIzero or \fBone\fP\fP consecutive occurrences of \fRpattern\fP  \fR!(pattern)\fP  Match \fIanything but\fP \fRpattern\fP  

.PP
\fBExamples\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const pm = require('picomatch');

// *(pattern) matches ZERO or more of "pattern"
console\&.log(pm\&.isMatch('a', 'a*(z)')); // true
console\&.log(pm\&.isMatch('az', 'a*(z)')); // true
console\&.log(pm\&.isMatch('azzz', 'a*(z)')); // true

// +(pattern) matches ONE or more of "pattern"
console\&.log(pm\&.isMatch('a', 'a*(z)')); // true
console\&.log(pm\&.isMatch('az', 'a*(z)')); // true
console\&.log(pm\&.isMatch('azzz', 'a*(z)')); // true

// supports multiple extglobs
console\&.log(pm\&.isMatch('foo\&.bar', '!(foo)\&.!(bar)')); // false

// supports nested extglobs
console\&.log(pm\&.isMatch('foo\&.bar', '!(!(foo))\&.!(!(bar))')); // true
.fi
.PP
.SS "POSIX brackets"
POSIX classes are disabled by default\&. Enable this feature by setting the \fRposix\fP option to true\&.

.PP
\fBEnable POSIX bracket support\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
console\&.log(pm\&.makeRe('[[:word:]]+', { posix: true }));
//=> /^(?:(?=\&.)[A\-Za\-z0\-9_]+\\/?)$/
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fBSupported POSIX classes\fP

.PP
The following named POSIX bracket expressions are supported:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[:alnum:]\fP - Alphanumeric characters, equ \fR[a-zA-Z0-9]\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[:alpha:]\fP - Alphabetical characters, equivalent to \fR[a-zA-Z]\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[:ascii:]\fP - ASCII characters, equivalent to \fR[\\\\x00-\\\\x7F]\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[:blank:]\fP - Space and tab characters, equivalent to \fR[ \\\\t]\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[:cntrl:]\fP - Control characters, equivalent to \fR[\\\\x00-\\\\x1F\\\\x7F]\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[:digit:]\fP - Numerical digits, equivalent to \fR[0-9]\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[:graph:]\fP - Graph characters, equivalent to \fR[\\\\x21-\\\\x7E]\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[:lower:]\fP - Lowercase letters, equivalent to \fR[a-z]\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[:print:]\fP - Print characters, equivalent to \fR[\\\\x20-\\\\x7E ]\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[:punct:]\fP - Punctuation and symbols, equivalent to \fR[\\\\-!"#$%&\\'()\\\\*+,\&./:;<=>?@[\\\\]^_\fP{|}~]\fR\&.\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fP[:space:]\fR - Extended space characters, equivalent to \fP[ \\t\\r\\n\\v\\f]\fR\&.\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fP[:upper:]\fR - Uppercase letters, equivalent to \fP[A-Z]\fR\&.\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fP[:word:]\fR - Word characters (letters, numbers and underscores), equivalent to \fP[A-Za-z0-9_]\fR\&.\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fP[:xdigit:]\fR - Hexadecimal digits, equivalent to \fP[A-Fa-f0-9]`\&.
.PP

.PP
See the \fRBash Reference Manual\fP for more information\&.
.SS "Braces"
Picomatch does not do brace expansion\&. For \fRbrace expansion\fP and advanced matching with braces, use \fRmicromatch\fP instead\&. Picomatch has very basic support for braces\&.
.SS "Matching special characters as literals"
If you wish to match the following special characters in a filepath, and you want to use these characters in your glob pattern, they must be escaped with backslashes or quotes:

.PP
\fBSpecial Characters\fP

.PP
Some characters that are used for matching in regular expressions are also regarded as valid file path characters on some platforms\&.

.PP
To match any of the following characters as literals: `$^*+?()[]

.PP
Examples:

.PP
.PP
.nf
console\&.log(pm\&.makeRe('foo/bar \\\\(1\\\\)'));
console\&.log(pm\&.makeRe('foo/bar \\\\(1\\\\)'));
.fi
.PP

